<v Matt Young>Throughout all of this, we were asking ourselves, like, in what ways could our own product help us do this better?

191
00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:56.100
<v Matt Young>And I've been through the days before Waterfall, the days when Waterfall was the methodology, Agile with both Scrum and Kanban.

192
00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:02.360
<v Matt Young>uses ShapeUp, the methodology from Basecamp to build its software. And the reason that we like

193
00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:08.400
<v Matt Young>that so much is that it does help you focus on that question that you asked, which is what is

194
00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:13.980
<v Matt Young>the next most important thing that we can do? So the first thing we did is we took our backlog,

195
00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:20.200
<v Matt Young>which had been built up to about 6,000 things, and we deleted it, all of it. It was just gone.

196
00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:25.980
<v Matt Young>Does that mean that those problems went away? It doesn't, but it keeps us from having to stare

197
00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:32.500
<v Matt Young>of convenient ideas that we can go for and forces us to really ask the question,

198
00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:36.860
<v Matt Young>how do we stay focused on what the most important thing is? So at the beginning of every cycle,

199
00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.440
<v Matt Young>we pull up a blank Google Doc where we put what are our goals as a company, and then we start

200
00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:47.400
<v Matt Young>listing ideas of improvements to the product that we think could help us reach those goals.

201
00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:53.000
<v Matt Young>We consult our own product, the UserVoice repo of feedback. We conduct interviews. We talk to

202
00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:56.880
<v Matt Young>our sales team, our marketing team, find out what they've learned, find out what they're hearing

203
00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:02.620
<v Matt Young>in demos. And it's very kind of up to the minute in our decision making. And we make plans and we

204
00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:07.400
<v Matt Young>write pitches that are oriented around problems to solve, not around solutions that are there.

205
00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:12.620
<v Matt Young>Get everyone to discuss those in great detail. And right up at the last minute, literally the

206
00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:17.520
<v Matt Young>week before we start another six-week development cycle, we decide which of those things is the most

207
00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:23.860
<v Matt Young>valuable to do. That has been a revolutionary shift for us to make. The adoption rates on

208
00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:28.880
<v Matt Young>new software that we put out over the past two years since we switched to do this are way higher

209
00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.540
<v Matt Young>than the ones that we had when we were still doing two-week sprints with Scrum.

210
00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:40.720
<v Noah Labhart>Let's switch to team then. So how did you go about building your team? And what did you look for in

211
00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:45.180
<v Noah Labhart>those people to indicate they were the winning horses to join you? I guess that could be when

212
00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.860
<v Noah Labhart>you joined or how you're doing it now.

213
00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.840
<v Matt Young>Human beings, I think, are inherently wired to enjoy

214
00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:54.280
<v Matt Young>enjoy predictability. You like showing up to work and you like knowing what's going to happen,

215
00:15:54.380 --> 00:16:00.920
<v Matt Young>but that's kind of at odds with making last minute decisions that are going to be the most valuable

216
00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:05.980
<v Matt Young>that are there. So we look for people who are really flexible and comfortable with imperfect

217
00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:12.220
<v Matt Young>information and really oriented around finding a way through what we don't know. And that

218
00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:16.720
<v Matt Young>necessitates tearing down walls in the organization, making sure that we don't have

219
00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:20.860
<v Matt Young>of a product team that's siloed from the engineering team, that we don't have a product

220
00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:25.940
<v Matt Young>and engineering organization in R&amp;D as a whole that is really removed from what people on the

221
00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:30.500
<v Matt Young>go-to-market side of the organization have. So we created regular meetings, for example,

222
00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:35.960
<v Matt Young>between the product team and the marketing team that seems obvious, but they hadn't been done

223
00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:43.020
<v Matt Young>before. So really looking for a team that is curious about how the work they do is either

224
00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:49.600
<v Matt Young>enhanced or affects other parts of the organization is critical. Flexibility is key. We look for

225
00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:55.720
<v Matt Young>people who are empathetic and kind, because I think to be successful in a high stress situation

226
00:16:55.720 --> 00:17:01.560
<v Matt Young>like that, you need to have those characteristics. And to me, that comes from the top down too. I

227
00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:08.220
<v Matt Young>can't be a bombastic leader and expect everyone else to remain cool, calm, and collected. So it's

228
00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:09.360
<v Matt Young>It's really important.

229
00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:11.420
<v Matt Young>We did some very tactical things too.

230
00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:13.660
<v Matt Young>I merged the product and engineering team.

231
00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.640
<v Matt Young>They both started reporting up to the same person.

232
00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:19.040
<v Matt Young>And we did the same thing with customer success, marketing, and sales.

233
00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:24.320
<v Matt Young>They all report up to the same person as well because they're ultimately responsible for growth.

234
00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:30.520
<v Matt Young>And doing that, I think, has created alignment and goals across the different teams as well.

235
00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.600
<v Noah Labhart>Let's flip to scalability then.

236
00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:39.840
<v Noah Labhart>So you mentioned some tech that you built or that the team used to build and make it scalable.

237
00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:46.020
<v Noah Labhart>But has it been scalable from day one or are you fighting this as you grow and gain traction in the product?

238
00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:57.900
<v Matt Young>When I showed up in 2015, the product was efficient and that in the traditional ways that you would create scalable technology with good database structure and indices, that worked well.

239
00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.860
<v Matt Young>But we were still operating on bare metal.

240
00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:05.960
<v Matt Young>We were hosting our servers at SoftLayer and deployments and builds were quite slow.

241
00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.940
<v Matt Young>It was difficult to iterate and roll back and all those things.

242
00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:13.280
<v Matt Young>This guy, Kurt Robinson, who's on our team, I shouldn't have said his name out loud because

243
00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:14.440
<v Matt Young>some of you are going to try to poach him.

244
00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:15.020
<v Matt Young>Don't do it.

245
00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:20.100
<v Matt Young>He came in in, gosh, I think this was 2016 or 17.

246
00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:21.600
<v Matt Young>He's been with us for a good long time.

247
00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:27.240
<v Matt Young>And in a matter of six months, had us moved over to the cloud, fully containerized with

248
00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:28.600
<v Matt Young>with Kubernetes orchestration,

249
00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:30.900
<v Matt Young>and that was really, really ahead of the game.

250
00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:33.980
<v Matt Young>That one change was a game changer for us

251
00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:37.020
<v Matt Young>because it lets you do things like spin up a new instance

252
00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:40.380
<v Matt Young>with just one change in the code base

253
00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.120
<v Matt Young>to test it independent of others with very little effort.

254
00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:47.300
<v Matt Young>It lets the rest of the team get involved

255
00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:49.860
<v Matt Young>in testing a feature and gets it out of the hands

256
00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.200
<v Matt Young>of just that engineer or that team of engineers

257
00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.180
<v Matt Young>that worked on it.

258
00:18:53.180 --> 00:19:07.480
<v Matt Young>And so now spinning up and spinning down resources in technology became a whole lot easier for us to the point where, you know, with GDPR coming out, we have a lot of customers outside of the US who are interested in having their system hosted in the EU.

259
00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:15.260
<v Matt Young>Piece of cake, you know, point our build scripts over to an EU data center and it took a day and there it is up and running.

260
00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:21.700
<v Matt Young>And in fact, we did our disaster recovery in the EU just to make sure that we could do that easily to make sure that it happened.

261
00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:30.220
<v Matt Young>From a people perspective, the scalability question is another one of those drivers that caused us to break down the walls between the organizations.

262
00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:38.320
<v Matt Young>We wanted to make sure that we didn't have the overhead in red tape or inefficiency in communication from team to team where they really needed to be working hand in hand.

263
00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:47.360
<v Matt Young>So by getting them working day to day and reporting to the same people and goals on the same things, that really created scalability in our people.

264
00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.820
<v Noah Labhart>As you step out on the balcony and you look across all that you've built, what are you most proud of?

265
00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:58.060
<v Matt Young>The team is the obvious number one.

266
00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:03.520
<v Matt Young>I don't know if you ever heard the adage like, you know, don't hire someone that you wouldn't want to invite over for dinner.

267
00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:11.320
<v Matt Young>But I would happily invite not only the people I work with, but their families and kids and all those people over for dinner at any time.

268
00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.680
<v Matt Young>They're just good people that are dedicated and smart.

269
00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:24.840
<v Matt Young>On a software and services perspective, the culture of being oriented around our customers and potential customers' problems is the thing I'm most proud of.

270
00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:28.160
<v Matt Young>The solutions will change month over month, year over year.

271
00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.740
<v Matt Young>Competition will come and go, etc.

272
00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:35.900
<v Matt Young>et cetera. But I think as long as we maintain the attitude that we are really trying to solve

273
00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:41.760
<v Matt Young>a real problem for our customers and not trying to convince them to buy software that wouldn't

274
00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:46.040
<v Matt Young>be good for them or railroad them into a particular methodology just because it's

275
00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:50.880
<v Matt Young>convenient for us is the recipe for an enduring business, not just one that's

276
00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.480
<v Matt Young>successful in the short term.

277
00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:55.420
<v Noah Labhart>Well, let's flip the script a little bit.

278
00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:59.600
<v Noah Labhart>Tell me about a mistake you made and how you and your team responded to it.

279
00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:04.960
<v Matt Young>business so long, I think we've made all the mistakes at this point, but we'll manage to

280
00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:11.900
<v Matt Young>unearth some more over the next several years. I think the mistake that I find ourselves

281
00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:20.280
<v Matt Young>tripping over repeatedly is when we try to build a function in-house that we really shouldn't be

282
00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:25.040
<v Matt Young>because it's not part of the unique solution offering of our product. A good example is

283
00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:30.340
<v Matt Young>billing and subscription management. We just got finished moving a homegrown internal billing system

284
00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:33.320
<v Matt Young>over to a third-party billing management system.

285
00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:37.620
<v Matt Young>We also use SaaSOptics to get reporting on everything that's there.

286
00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:46.320
<v Matt Young>And it didn't occur to me until we had done that how blind I was to the ebb and flow of our customer base

287
00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:51.840
<v Matt Young>and the minutia of people expanding and contracting their subscription relationship with us.

288
00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:57.780
<v Matt Young>That big picture of our business as a whole was spread across multiple systems for a while.

289
00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:07.240
<v Matt Young>But that was all a symptom of the fact that we felt like we needed to build these things ourselves or that it would be too much effort to move to a third party system.

290
00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:19.280
<v Matt Young>So, yeah, one of the we keep using vendors and I think the efficiency of building SaaS products is built on the effective use of open source software and the effective use of third party vendors.

291
00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:26.240
<v Matt Young>None of us would think of hosting servers ourselves anymore in small to medium scale SaaS systems.

292
00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:32.320
<v Matt Young>you build a billing system? Why would you build email delivery or notification systems yourself?

293
00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:38.020
<v Matt Young>Using third parties for that has been something that we relate to, but is something that we'll

294
00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:40.580
<v Matt Young>have our eye out on for the foreseeable future.

295
00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.580
<v Noah Labhart>Well, what does the future look like for the

296
00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:45.760
<v Noah Labhart>product and for your team?

297
00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:51.100
<v Matt Young>We're in the middle right now of touching the third rail of the

298
00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:58.320
<v Matt Young>the product. There's a centralized place in our product where about 70% of our customers' time is

299
00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:02.020
<v Matt Young>spent. And that's always been a place that's a little bit scary to touch, but we're going through

300
00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:09.380
<v Matt Young>a necessary revamp of that in order to modernize the workflows that people want. UserVoice is a

