00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:49.200
<v Matt Young>more likely selling with other teams as a more holistic solution.

309
00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:55.720
<v Noah Labhart>Let's switch to you, Matt. Who influences the way that you work? Name a person you look up to and why.

310
00:23:56.780 --> 00:24:03.880
<v Matt Young>That's a really hard question for me because I think that I try to avoid emulating any one individual.

311
00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:11.660
<v Matt Young>And I try to find in anyone that I meet aspects of their personality that I'm a little bit jealous of and would like to have for myself.

312
00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:16.320
<v Matt Young>I kind of want to answer that question in the opposite way.

313
00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:40.560
<v Matt Young>One of the easiest ways to become a better leader is to drop habits that you don't think are productive or valuable that you've seen in the people that you've worked with before. Rather than me trying to be Jeff Bezos or Elon Musk or anyone who's a multi-billionaire with global influence, it's easier for me to affect personal improvement by saying, okay, well, I don't like it when people yell. I don't like it when people raise their voices.

314
00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:48.540
<v Matt Young>I don't like it when people use words that are belittling or don't pay attention to how people's personal lives affect their work and things like that.

315
00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:58.900
<v Matt Young>And having been witness to that or victim of it myself, I try to drop those negative habits and encourage that in my leadership team and anyone who manages at UserVoice.

316
00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:08.900
<v Matt Young>And I think overall, that's the way to create more incremental improvement in leadership rather than aspiring to an almost impossible to achieve lofty goal.

317
00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.820
<v Noah Labhart>So then we talked about a mistake, right?

318
00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:14.040
<v Noah Labhart>But a little bit different spin.

319
00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.820
<v Noah Labhart>If you could go back to the beginning, what would you do differently?

320
00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:19.120
<v Noah Labhart>Or where would you consider taking a different approach?

321
00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.360
<v Noah Labhart>And that can be with UserVoice or really anything in your career.

322
00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:29.940
<v Matt Young>This may be a common thing, but one of the hardest things that anyone in a leadership position needs to do.

323
00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:37.980
<v Matt Young>I think anyone who has hiring and firing rights, anyone who has budgetary responsibilities in their organization,

324
00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:42.400
<v Matt Young>whenever you know that a change that you're going to make is going to negatively impact

325
00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:45.840
<v Matt Young>someone financially you know you're going to lay them off they're going to lose their job etc

326
00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:51.840
<v Matt Young>most people try to avoid the discomfort of that situation and every time I've needed to make

327
00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:57.160
<v Matt Young>personnel change and I reflect on it I realized that you know I knew that I needed to make that

328
00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:05.300
<v Matt Young>change months ago and I was just avoiding the short-term pain of executing that so I always

329
00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:12.900
<v Matt Young>I always wish that I was more honest with myself when I recognize that something isn't working right and recognizing that it's going to be uncomfortable to change it.

330
00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:16.680
<v Matt Young>But just jumping into it, it's, you know, this is not life and death.

331
00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:22.180
<v Matt Young>Yes, laying someone off is a big deal to them, and I want to make sure that they land on their feet.

332
00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:34.900
<v Matt Young>But if the primary responsibility is to the organization and the rest of the people who are reliant on the company being successful for their livelihoods, it just becomes a necessary thing to do.

333
00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:42.060
<v Matt Young>So the flip side of the coin of being highly empathetic is that it's very hard to do things that can hurt people.

334
00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:47.160
<v Matt Young>But recognizing that it's a necessary part of the job, that's something I wish I'd learned a little bit earlier.

335
00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:50.520
<v Noah Labhart>Well, last question, Matt.

336
00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:55.500
<v Noah Labhart>So you're getting on a plane and you're sitting next to a young entrepreneur who's built the next big thing.

337
00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:56.580
<v Noah Labhart>They're jazzed about it.

338
00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:59.600
<v Noah Labhart>They can't wait to show it off to the world and can't wait to show it off to you right there on the plane.

339
00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:03.560
<v Noah Labhart>What advice do you give that person having gone down this road a bit?

340
00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:10.920
<v Matt Young>that I'm immediately jealous of them. People who are that confident to move forward and build

341
00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:17.040
<v Matt Young>something and they've done it and they're excited about it, that is hard energy to manufacture if it

342
00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:21.620
<v Matt Young>isn't part of your personality. The one thing I want to make sure that people do is that they have

343
00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:27.540
<v Matt Young>really vetted the problem, that they can articulate very clearly what the problem is and why it is

344
00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:32.360
<v Matt Young>valuable to solve rather than focusing on the solution itself. I think that's the place where

345
00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:37.300
<v Matt Young>where most, whether it's a whole product or just a feature within a product, when there's a miss,

346
00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:43.580
<v Matt Young>it's because it was a tool that you could build rather than a tool that you needed to build.

347
00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:48.300
<v Matt Young>And in order for people to be successful, they really need to market it. They need to orient

348
00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:52.760
<v Matt Young>the product around it. It's got to be oriented around the problem that it's actually solving.

349
00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:57.680
<v Matt Young>And you need to be able to say it to people in a way that they kind of immediately understand.

